.TH SMTIMP 1
.SH NAME
smtimp \- what the features are worth to the learners
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smtimp
[
.B \-o
.I dir
]
[
.B \-n
.I repeats
]
[
.B \-j
.I threads
]
[
.B \-r
.I seed
]
[
.B \-t
.I seconds
]
[
.I dataset.tsv
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I smtimp
reads a dataset as
.IR smtdata (1)
writes one, from the file named or from the standard input, takes the settings
.IR smtfit (1)
chose out of
.I dir
.RB ( \-o ,
.B out
by default),
deals the rows as its seed
.RB ( \-r )
deals them, and writes two tables on the worth of the features back into
.IR dir .
.PP
.B imp.tsv
is by permutation: a feature is shuffled among the validation rows, and the
importance of the feature is the score the setting then loses, over
.I repeats
shufflings
.RB ( \-n ,
five by default).
The forest and the boosting are asked.
The counts of a formula move together with its size, and a row with one of
them shuffled is unlike any row the forest ever saw: so the table costs every
feature about as much where they move together, and says more of what
recognizes a family than of what tells the time.
.PP
.B ablate.tsv
is by ablation, and replaces it.
The features fall into six groups:
.B size
(the bytes and the counts of things),
.B width
(the bitvector widths of the variables),
.B depth ,
.B product
(the multiplicative and division operators),
.B array ,
and
.BR operators ,
the counts of all the other operators.
The forest, and a plane beside it (ridge over the times, logistic over the
outcome), are each taught over one group alone and over all the features but
that group, at the setting chosen, and scored on validation: the error of the
time, or the mean F1.
.B with
is the score over the group alone,
.B without
the score over all but it, and the line
.B all
holds the whole.
.PP
The fits run side by side on
.I threads
.RB ( \-j ,
one a processor by default).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
smtimp \-o out dataset.tsv
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR smtfit (1),
.IR smtcal (1)
